{"name":"Vapour Blue","hex":"#bebdbd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vapour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["British standard spelling of vapor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["British standard spelling of vapor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vapour"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vapor","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, a vapor or vapour is a substance in the gas phase at a temperature lower than its critical temperature, which means that the vapor can be condensed to a liquid by increasing the pressure on it without reducing the temperature of the vapor. A vapor is different from an aerosol. An aerosol is a suspension of tiny particles of liquid, solid, or both within a gas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Nitrogen_dioxide_gas.jpg/330px-Nitrogen_dioxide_gas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vapor"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/500px-Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/1280px-Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/500px-Vapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"If the vapor pressure exceeds the equilibrium value, it becomes supersaturated and condenses on any available nucleation sites e. g. particles of dust. This principle is used in cloud chambers, where particles of radiation are visualized because they nucleate formation of water droplets.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVapor_being_used_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Crepuscular_Rays_Beam_through_the_Mist_Blown_from_Takkakaw_Falls.jpg/500px-Crepuscular_Rays_Beam_through_the_Mist_Blown_from_Takkakaw_Falls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Crepuscular_Rays_Beam_through_the_Mist_Blown_from_Takkakaw_Falls.jpg/1280px-Crepuscular_Rays_Beam_through_the_Mist_Blown_from_Takkakaw_Falls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co